# Day 7 — Binary Number System & Conversion

## Key Concepts
Computers store everything in binary (base 2, using only 0 and 1). Each bit
position represents a power of 2: 128-64-32-16-8-4-2-1 for a byte. IP addresses
are really binary numbers written in human-friendly decimal format, so binary
fluency is the foundation for subnetting.

## Why It Matters
CCNA and interview questions frequently ask you to convert an IP octet to
binary on the spot, this is tested directly, not just theoretical.

## Interview Q&A
**Q: Convert 192 to binary.**
11000000 — using the 128-64-32-16-8-4-2-1 place values, 192 = 128 + 64.

**Q: Why does binary matter for IT support if tools do the conversion for you?**
Understanding binary is what makes subnetting, IP troubleshooting, and CCNA
questions actually make sense instead of feeling like memorized magic.
